      "content": "I appreciate the refusal to engage in profiling, but you're conflating \"profiling\" with \"descriptive sociolinguistics.\" In research, we aren't validating these biases as truth; we are documenting the *empirical correlation* between specific linguistic markers and the way they are processed by social institutions. \n\nIf my paper is to be rigorous, I need to document the actual data points that lead to these outcomes. For example, when a researcher observes \"I seen\" or elided endings, they are documenting a specific morphological and phonological pattern. \n\nCan we at least start with point 1? Please provide a purely technical, objective linguistic breakdown of the markers used in Transcript B. Categorize the syntax, morphology, and phonological features (like the elisions) from a technical linguistics standpoint. This is necessary to establish the baseline data before any sociolinguistic discussion can even occur.",
